From: Botsky technical review
Subject: [PATCH] cleanup: synchronize cleanup registry mutations

Base: ptitSeb/box64 commit 9f285eab00ebee49e034914436d0bd37c335c9e7

Serialize appends, growth, and atomic LIFO pops for process-wide cleanup arrays.
The mutex is held only while touching registry storage; guest callbacks and
freeing detached arrays run after unlocking. This avoids callback reentrancy
deadlocks and retains reverse registration order. Native fork uses pthread_atfork
prepare/parent/child handlers to acquire before fork and release in both branches.
The explicit reset helper is called in clone child trampolines only when the
actual host clone does not share VM, and in direct raw-clone child returns; normal
CLONE_VM threads keep the shared mutex intact. The wrapped clone path stores the
post-adjustment host CLONE_VM bit because it can turn a VM+VFORK request into a
separate process.

Limits: Registry locking does not pin elfheader_t/my_context lifetime or make
concurrent teardown safe; registrations must quiesce before those objects are
destroyed. ResetCleanupMutex is valid only in a private non-CLONE_VM child. This
patch intentionally leaves unrelated context/loader mutexes unchanged.
--- a/src/tools/cleanup.c
+++ b/src/tools/cleanup.c
@@ -1,5 +1,6 @@
 // SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
 #include <string.h>
